(* ============================================================
   musics.ebnf — Instaparse grammar for the musics DSL
   ============================================================
   Inspired by LilyPond but this is a custom DSL.

    Bracket system -- deliberately a superset of LilyPond's own, not a
    parallel one: { } is the ONLY bracket for a music-expression body
    anywhere (a Sequence, \times/\tuplet/\transpose's body, a VarDef's
    value), exactly like real LilyPond, which has no separate "scope"
    delimiter of its own either -- myVar = { c4 d4 }, \times 2/3 { c8 d8
    e8 } are real LilyPond spellings, not this DSL's invention. ( ) is
    reserved for exactly what LilyPond itself uses it for and nothing
    else: a slur mark glued onto a Note/Chord, e.g. c4( d4 e4).
     << >>    Parallel
     { }      Sequence     (also: \times/\tuplet/\transpose's body, a
                             VarDef's value -- same rule, reused as-is;
                             the walker decides whether a given { }
                             becomes a real, addressable container or is
                             spliced/stashed, based on WHERE it sits,
                             never on how it's spelled)
     ^{ }     Context      (named context / envelope definition)
     '{ }     Unit         (grouped elements, no context of its own --
                             a genuine extension beyond LilyPond, which
                             has no equivalent, so it doesn't reuse { })
     [ ]      Data
     @{ }     ElementAlgo  (algo over elements)
     @[ ]     AtomicAlgo   (algo over data)

   Id        = Name ':'    definition: registers in repo
   Reference = ':' Name    reference:  looks up in repo
   VarDef    = Name '=' { ... }    definition: stashed by the walker
   VarRef    = '\' Name            reference:  spliced in by the walker
               (must be defined before referenced -- see VarDef below)

   A trailing '(' / ')' glued directly onto a Note/Chord (no space) is a
   slur mark -- LilyPond-style, e.g. c4( d4 e4) -- ( ) never means
   anything else in this grammar.
   ============================================================ *)

(* VarDef is only ever reachable here, directly in Program's own top-
   level element list -- not through Element/ParElement, so it can never
   appear nested inside a Sequence/Parallel/Unit/etc. body. Same
   restriction LilyPond itself has (a variable is defined before the
   music, not inside it) -- and it matters beyond style: VarDef being
   reachable everywhere Element is meant a typo anywhere in a Sequence
   body (e.g. "cc4") could make instaparse's furthest-failure tracking
   follow a dead-end "maybe this is a variable definition" attempt right
   past the real mistake, reporting a confusing "expected =" instead of
   pointing at the actual typo. Confirmed directly, not assumed --
   {verse: cc4 d4} used to fail with a useless "Expected one of: =,
   comment..." at column 13, nowhere near the actual double-pitch-letter
   mistake at column 9. *)
Program = ws? (TopElement (ws TopElement)*)? ws?
(* TopElement deliberately excludes Instruction, the transient half of
   Command (transpose/times/tuplet/grace), and, as of this rule's final
   form, Leaf/Reference/VarRef too (the other three alternatives of
   Part, alongside Composite). All of these share one root cause: they
   can write directly into whatever context is on top of the builder
   stack, and at Program's own top level, before any real container has
   been entered, that's :ROOT itself -- meant to be a read-only endpoint
   with a guaranteed value for every key (see common.defaults/
   root-defaults, core.domain.context/context-root).
   - Instruction (BangConst/KeyAssignment/Assignment/Invalidate/
     Partial) has no container of its own at all -- it always
     targets current-context directly. Confirmed live: a bare top-level
     !vol<...!vol> landed a genuine multi-point ramp on :ROOT's own
     "volume" envelope.
   - transpose/times/tuplet/grace DO get their own context while being
     built, but it's transient -- flat-core-builder/pop-container
     replays every instruction written inside them onto whatever's
     already on the stack once their wrapper is spliced away, same
     "current-context" target Instruction itself writes to. Also
     confirmed live: \\times 2/3 {!vol:30<l c4 d4}, bare at the top
     level, replayed straight onto :ROOT the same way.
   - A bare Leaf/Chord with a note-glued dynamic or hairpin (c4\\f) --
     ordinary, common surface syntax, not some obscure corner --
     resolves through the exact same current-context write
     (flat-tree-walker/apply-note-dynamics!) as a standalone !f would.
     Confirmed live too (a NullPointerException trying to append a
     second point onto :ROOT's now-bare-constant value, before this
     rule closed the gap -- see core.domain.context's ValueSource).
   - A bare Reference (:name), when it resolves to a ^{ } :CONTEXT
     block, and a bare VarRef (\\name) both replay a stashed envelope
     onto current-context the same way (apply-context-ref/walk-var-ref)
     -- same underlying mechanism, two more triggers of it.
   repeat/tremolo are the one exception kept reachable here: unlike
   everything above, CLAUDE.md already documents these as NOT transient
   -- \\repeat's own body and measured tremolo's are real, retained
   containers (an Iterator's own :source/:alternative), each with a
   genuine context of their own that instructions written inside it
   target directly, never replayed onto a parent at all -- exactly as
   safe at top level as any other real container (Composite). The
   practical result: every top-level program now needs at least one
   real wrapping container -- a bare, un-nested phrase like `c4 d4 e4`
   is no longer valid Program text on its own, only inside a { }/<< >>/
   etc. (or a \\repeat/\\tremolo). *)
<TopElement> = Composite | repeat | tremolo | VarDef

(* Element: musical parts, instructions, commands *)
<Element> = Part | Instruction | Command

(* Part: a composite pattern of musical data *)
<Part> = Composite | Leaf | Reference | VarRef
<Leaf> = Chord | Note | Rest | Drum | MultiRest

(* Bar / section markers -- pure structural signals for a future conductor,
   no effect on musical timing. Four levels of granularity, finest to
   coarsest: | (bar/measure) || ||| |||| (progressively larger parts).
   Reachable only through Sep below (Sequence/Parallel element lists), not
   through Element/Leaf/Instruction -- so it never needs surrounding
   whitespace: c4|d4, c4 |d4, c4| d4, c4 | d4 are all one bar line. *)
BarLine = #'\|{1,4}'

(* Element-list separator for Sequence/Parallel: a plain gap (ws, possibly
   with comments) or a bar line with optional whitespace on either side.
   Hidden rule -- a BarLine match bubbles up as a normal sibling in the
   parent's children, same as any other hidden-rule passthrough. *)
<Sep> = ws
      | ws? BarLine ws?

(* A bar line at the very start or end of a Sequence/Parallel body -- e.g.
   a closing bar before the ']', or a pickup bar before the first note.
   Always requires at least the BarLine itself (unlike Sep, never just ws),
   so it can't collide with the plain ws? already flanking the body. *)
<EdgeBar> = ws? BarLine ws?

(* ── Composites ─────────────────────────────────────────── *)

<Composite> = Context | Sequence | Parallel | Unit | Data | AtomicAlgo | ElementAlgo

Context     = <'^{'> ws? (Id ws)?  Instruction (ws Instruction)* ws? <'}'>
            | <'^{'> ws? (Id ws)                                 ws? <'}'>

Sequence    = <'{'>   ws? (Id ws)? EdgeBar? Element (Sep Element)* EdgeBar? ws? <'}'>
            | <'{'>   ws? (Id ws)                                          ws? <'}'>

(* ParElement's alternatives (Context/Sequence/Reference/Instruction/
   Command) all start with a distinctive non-alphanumeric character
   (^ { : ! \), so two of them can sit directly adjacent with no
   separator at all -- Sep is optional here, not just the bar line
   covered by EdgeBar. *)
Parallel    = <'<<'>  ws? (Id ws)? EdgeBar? ParElement (Sep? ParElement)* EdgeBar? ws? <'>>'>
            | <'<<'>  ws? (Id ws)                                                 ws? <'>>'>

(* Unit: an ordered group of Elements that shares its enclosing
   container's context instead of carrying one of its own -- a shuffling/
   randomizing algorithm can reorder Units within a Sequence and the notes
   inside each one travel together. Same body shape as Sequence
   (including bar lines), just on its own bracket -- Unit IS a real,
   addressable container (it keeps an id, appears in :children like any
   Sequence, just without its own :context), unlike \times/\tuplet/
   \transpose's body or a VarDef's value, which reuse Sequence's own { }
   directly and are spliced/stashed rather than registered -- Unit gets
   its own bracket precisely because it doesn't share that fate. *)
Unit        = <"'{">  ws? (Id ws)? EdgeBar? Element (Sep Element)* EdgeBar? ws? <'}'>
            | <"'{">  ws? (Id ws)                                          ws? <'}'>

Data        = <'['>   ws? (type ws)? (DataElement (ws DataElement)*)? ws? <']'>

(* AtomicAlgo's own arg list -- a Data literal (walked to a plain value
   seq), a bare Primitive (walked to a single scalar), or another
   AtomicAlgo call, genuinely recursive: an Arg can itself be
   @[ someAlgo ... ], its own result passed through to the outer call
   exactly as returned, no flattening -- e.g. a combinator algo (a "zip",
   say) fed by two nested calls, one generating pitches and one
   generating durations. Freely mixed with Data/Primitive args too, in
   whatever order the target fn's own params expect (see
   flat-tree-walker/run-algo and input.algo-registry's own namespace
   docstring for the full calling contract). *)
AtomicAlgo  = <'@['>  ws? (algo ws) (AlgoArg (ws AlgoArg)*)? ws? <']'>
<AlgoArg>   = Data | Primitive | AtomicAlgo

(* ElementAlgo's own arg list -- zero or more bare Primitives (Int/Float/
   Ratio) first, e.g. a split-count or voice-index, THEN the Elements
   themselves (real Leaf/Rest/Drum/nested-Composite content, walked the
   same as any other container's body) -- e.g. @{ split 2 0 c4 d4 e4 }.
   Primitive is deliberately the only scalar shape allowed here, not the
   fuller AlgoArg (which also allows Data/AtomicAlgo) AtomicAlgo's own
   Args use -- Data is already reachable as an ordinary Element (Part
   includes Composite, which includes Data), so admitting it here too
   would make a leading [ ... ] genuinely ambiguous between "a scalar
   arg" and "the first Element of the body", exactly the kind of
   ambiguity CLAUDE.md's "Comments and variables" section warns
   instaparse resolves unreliably. A bare Primitive has no such
   collision -- no Element alternative (Leaf/Instruction/Command/
   Reference/VarRef) ever accepts a bare, unattached number. *)
ElementAlgo = <'@{'>  ws? (algo ws) (Primitive ws)* (Element (ws Element)*)? ws? <'}'>

type = Name
(* algo has its own token, not the shared Name (whose regex forbids a
   hyphen -- CLAUDE.md's "Grammar" section covers why compound tempo
   markings needed a camelCase alias for the same reason, BangConst's
   Name token being hyphen-free): an algo name commonly *is* a Clojure
   fn's own kebab-case symbol name (algo.common.isorhythm/color-talea,
   say), and forcing every registered algorithm through a camelCase
   alias just to be callable as @[ name ... ] (the way "colorTalea"
   needed one) is exactly the friction this widening removes --
   @[ color-talea ... ] can now match the underlying fn's name
   directly, no translation required. *)
algo = AlgoName
AlgoName = #'[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9_-]*'

(* ParElement: like Element but without bare Leafs. Unit is deliberately
   excluded -- its purpose is preserving grouping under reordering, and
   Parallel's children have no sequential order to reorder in the first
   place (they're simultaneous, not sequential), so there's no use case
   for it here. VarDef is excluded too, same as Element -- see the note
   by Program's own definition. VarRef stays -- referencing a variable
   inside a Parallel is unaffected, only defining one is restricted. *)
<ParElement> = Context | Sequence | Reference | Instruction | Command | VarRef

(* DataElement: element for data containers *)
<ExtendedDataElement> = Atom | Primitive | Keyword | Name
<DataElement>         = Atom | Primitive
<Atom>                = Pitch | Duration | BareDuration | Articulation | BangConst | StringLit
<Primitive>           = Ratio | Float | Int

(* ── Commands ───────────────────────────────────────────── *)

<Command> = transpose | times | tuplet | repeat | tremolo | grace

(* Transpose *)
(* The ws before the body is optional (ws?), not mandatory -- unlike the
   ws between the command word and its own arguments, there's nothing
   here that could actually collide with the body's leading '{':
   to-pitch is a bare Pitch (no NoteSuffix*, so SlurMark's glued '('
   never even applies, and it's moot anyway since '{' isn't '(' ), and
   neither OctaveTicks (' | ,) nor OctaveAbs's digit regex can produce a
   '{' either, so `\transpose c g'{\melody}` parses exactly the same as
   `\transpose c g' {\melody}`. *)
transpose  = #'\\transpose' ws from-pitch ws to-pitch ws? Sequence
from-pitch = Pitch
to-pitch   = Pitch

(* Tuplets -- same optional-ws reasoning as transpose above: a Ratio's
   digits can't produce the body's leading '{' either. Body reuses
   Sequence's own { } directly, same as VarDef's value -- see the
   top-of-file bracket-system comment for why this is a real LilyPond
   spelling (\times 2/3 { c8 d8 e8 }), not this DSL's own invention;
   flat-core-builder/pop-container still splices the children straight
   into the parent rather than registering an id, exactly as it always
   did, regardless of which bracket wrote them. *)
times  = #'\\times'  ws multiply-factor ws? Sequence
tuplet = #'\\tuplet' ws divide-factor   ws? Sequence

multiply-factor = Ratio
divide-factor   = Ratio

(* Repeats *)
repeat = #'\\repeat' ws repeat-type ws repeats ws Element (ws volta)?
<repeat-type> = 'volta' | 'unfold'
volta   = #'\\alternative' ws Sequence
repeats = Int

(* Measured tremolo -- single note/chord tremolo is a NoteSuffix *)
tremolo = #'\\repeat' ws 'tremolo' ws divisor ws Sequence
divisor = Int

(* Grace Notes
   All five forms now capture two Elements: the grace note and the main
   note it borrows time from. Order differs by keyword:
     \grace/\acciaccatura/\appoggiatura/\slashedGrace  -- grace-note main-note
     \afterGrace                                       -- main-note grace-note *)
grace = #'\\grace'        ws Element ws Element
      | #'\\acciaccatura' ws Element ws Element
      | #'\\appoggiatura' ws Element ws Element
      | #'\\slashedGrace' ws Element ws Element
      | #'\\afterGrace'   ws Element ws Element

(* ── Variables ──────────────────────────────────────────── *)
(* name = ( ... ) defines a variable; \name references it. Both are real
   grammar constructs (unlike the old text-level extract-vars/expand-vars
   pre-processing this replaces) -- parsed as part of the original text,
   so nothing about var handling can ever shift a later parse error's
   line/column relative to what was actually written. flat-tree-walker
   resolves both in a single top-to-bottom walk: a VarDef's value is
   walked and stashed (name -> its already-built children + context) the
   moment it's reached, and a VarRef splices that stashed content in --
   which is why a variable must be defined before it's referenced (same
   rule LilyPond itself uses), not just conventionally but structurally:
   nothing is stashed yet on a first pass, there is no first pass.

   The value is always a Sequence ({ }) -- the exact same rule
   \times/\tuplet/\transpose's own body reuses, and the real LilyPond
   spelling for a variable's value (myVar = { c4 d4 }, never "whatever
   text is left on the line" -- LilyPond has no such construct, see the
   top-of-file bracket-system comment). Grammar-checked the moment it's
   written, whether or not it's ever referenced, same as before this
   reused Sequence instead of a dedicated Scope rule -- the walker is
   what decides a VarDef's own { } gets stashed into var-map rather than
   registered as an addressable container, based on it sitting in
   VarDef's own value position, not on how it was spelled.

   VarName excludes every reserved command/ornament word (transpose,
   times, tuplet, repeat, alternative, grace and its four synonyms, and
   all 17 ornament names) -- not by convention but because instaparse's own
   ambiguity resolution for an otherwise-genuinely-ambiguous grammar is
   not reliable declaration order (verified directly against instaparse
   itself, not assumed), so a bare \trill has to be structurally
   incapable of also parsing as VarRef, not just conventionally
   discouraged from being used that way. Applied to VarDef's own name
   too, not just VarRef -- so defining a variable named e.g. trill is a
   parse error immediately, rather than a silently unreachable
   definition (since \trill could then only ever mean the ornament). *)
VarDef = VarName ws? <'='> ws? Sequence
VarRef = <'\\'> VarName

VarName = #'(?!(prallmordent|prallprall|lineprall|prallup|pralldown|downprall|prall|upmordent|downmordent|mordent|trill|turn|reverseturn|verylongfermata|longfermata|shortfermata|fermata|transpose|times|tuplet|repeat|alternative|acciaccatura|appoggiatura|slashedGrace|afterGrace|grace|time|tempo|key)\b)[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9_]*'

(* ── Instructions ───────────────────────────────────────── *)

(* SlurStart/SlurEnd (!( / !)) are gone -- they were a second, non-
   LilyPond spelling for exactly what the note-glued SlurMark (c4( d4
   e4), see below) already does: both ultimately just set/clear the
   walker's own :in-slur? flag (flat-tree-walker/walk-note et al), so
   removing the standalone !-prefixed form loses no real capability,
   only a redundant, non-conformant way to spell it. *)
<Instruction> = BangConst | KeyAssignment | Assignment | Invalidate | Partial
              | Time | Tempo | Key

(* \partial <duration> -- LilyPond's own literal spelling (\partial 8),
   not a !-prefixed Assignment like !Meter:/!tempo: -- every other
   Instruction in this grammar sets an ordinary, ramping-capable context
   value, but \partial is a pure structural declaration (affects bar-
   boundary accounting only, never a sounding duration) with no real
   LilyPond equivalent among this DSL's own !key:value instructions, so
   it keeps LilyPond's own backslash-command spelling instead of being
   folded into that family just for consistency. Walked to a plain,
   :fixed context value under :Partial (core.domain.resolve/
   common-keys+defaults samples it per leaf, same batched pass :Meter
   already rides in) -- a voice applies it ONCE, against whatever's
   active on its own very first leaf, to seed where its first :bar
   crossing actually falls (see core.async-engine's own comment on
   :partial-pending?). *)
Partial = <'\\partial'> ws Duration

(* \time / \tempo / \key -- LilyPond's own free-standing backslash-
   command spellings for exactly what !Meter:/!tempo:/!key: (see
   KeyAssignment below and Assignment's own Value alternatives) already
   set -- added as alternative surface syntax alongside the existing
   !-prefixed forms, not a replacement for them, same relationship
   \partial above already has to a same-effect !-prefixed instruction
   (there is none for \partial, but the pattern -- LilyPond's own
   spelling accepted as-is, wired to the same context-setting machinery
   -- is the same). All three needed "time"/"tempo"/"key" added to
   VarName's own exclusion list above: unlike \partial (whose body,
   Duration, can never also start a valid standalone Element, so
   \partial 8 was never genuinely ambiguous against VarRef("partial") +
   a separate Element), \key's own body starts with a bare pitch letter
   (\key d \major could otherwise also parse as VarRef("key") +
   Note("d") + VarRef("major") -- three independently valid Elements in
   a row) -- a real, demonstrable collision, not a hypothetical one, so
   this exclusion is load-bearing the same way the ornament-name
   exclusions already there are. *)

(* \time N/D -- reuses walk-assignment's own :Ratio case (el/parse-
   meter-str) via a shared walker helper, the same conversion !Meter:N/D
   already goes through. No quoted-additive-grouping form of its own --
   real LilyPond's \time doesn't have one either; write !Meter:"N/D(a+b+c)"
   directly if an explicit grouping is needed. *)
Time = <'\\time'> ws Ratio

(* \tempo <TempoMark|Int> -- reuses the exact TempoMark conversion
   walk-assignment's own :TempoMark case already applies
   (el/tempo->quarter-bpm), plus a bare Int (\tempo 120, quarter-note
   BPM implied, mirroring !tempo:120's own bare-BPM form). LilyPond also
   allows a free-text label before the numeric mark (\tempo "Andante"
   4=120) -- not implemented, since this DSL has no printed-score output
   for a text label to annotate. *)
Tempo = <'\\tempo'> ws (TempoMark | Int)

(* \key <pitch> \<mode> -- reuses el/parse-key, the same conversion
   KeyAssignment's own walker uses, via a tonic string built from
   KeyPitch. The pitch is written LOWERCASE and language-aware (read
   through whichever \language is active, same table an ordinary note's
   own Accidental resolves against -- see language-for-mode) and the
   mode is its own backslash-prefixed word, exactly real LilyPond's own
   \key d \major spelling -- structurally different from KeyAssignment's
   !key:D.major (uppercase letter, dotted mode suffix, symbolic
   accidental only). KeyPitch is deliberately narrower than the ordinary
   Pitch rule (PitchLetterRel Accidental? only, no octave) -- a key's
   tonic has no octave, in LilyPond or here. ModeName is restricted to
   the modes common.music-elements/scale-steps actually defines (see
   "Meter and indispensability" in the project root CLAUDE.md) rather
   than reusing the unrestricted Name token, so an unsupported mode word
   is a parse error at the point it's written, not a silently-nil key
   discovered only at playback. *)
Key      = <'\\key'> ws KeyPitch ws <'\\'> ModeName
KeyPitch = PitchLetterRel Accidental?
ModeName = 'harmonic-minor' | 'melodic-minor' | 'major' | 'minor' | 'ionian'
         | 'dorian' | 'phrygian' | 'lydian' | 'mixolydian' | 'aeolian' | 'locrian'

BangConst     = <'!'> Name
KeyAssignment = <'!key:'> KeySpec
(* Assignment excludes the literal name "key" via AssignName, same
   negative-lookahead pattern VarName uses to exclude reserved words
   above -- for the same reason: instaparse's ambiguity resolution
   between this rule and KeyAssignment is not reliable declaration
   order (confirmed directly: !key:Bb.major, whose tonic has no '#'
   to force KeySpec, parsed as this rule instead of KeyAssignment
   before this exclusion existed -- only a tonic with a literal '#'
   was ever unambiguous, since Name's plain-letter regex can't match
   it). Without the exclusion, !key:<tonic> is genuinely ambiguous
   whenever the tonic is letters-only, and which rule wins isn't
   something to depend on. *)
(* Value RampMark? (!vol:mf<) is deliberately NOT mirrored onto BangConst
   (!mf< would NOT mean the same thing) -- Name has no exclusion list, so
   a trailing RampMark there would collide with this very rule's own
   bare-Ramp alternative: !p< already parses today as
   Assignment(AssignName "p") + Ramp(bare "<") -- "p" is a registered
   :panning alias -- and "p" is also a real DynamicMark word (pianissimo),
   so BangConst("p") + RampMark would be directly, demonstrably
   ambiguous with it, not just hypothetically. Confirmed directly against
   the grammar, not assumed -- same kind of instaparse-ambiguity-
   resolution-isn't-reliable situation as the KeyAssignment note below. *)
Assignment    = <'!'> AssignName Ramp
              | <'!'> AssignName <':'> Value RampMark?

AssignName = #'(?!key\b)[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9_]*'
(* Invalidate: counterpart of ! -- !mf sets a context value, !/mf marks
   it no-longer-active from here on, so the search falls through to
   whatever the enclosing context would say instead (see
   core.domain.context/ctx-invalidate). Unambiguous vs. BangConst/
   Assignment: Name never starts with '/', so '!/mf' can only ever be
   this rule, never '!' + a Name reading "/mf". *)
Invalidate    = <'!/'> Name

(* SignedFloat/SignedInt (not the plain Float/Int used everywhere else --
   durations, octaves, repeat/divisor counts, a Ramp's own DurationExpr --
   none of which should ever go negative) are the only numeric literals
   that accept an explicit leading sign, used just here and in Target
   below. A context value genuinely can be negative (panning -1.0..1.0,
   transposition, bend, micro-timing, ...) -- an explicit '+' is accepted
   too (Integer/parseInt and Double/parseDouble already handle it
   natively, no walker change needed), purely for symmetry with '-' when
   an author wants to write it explicitly (e.g. alternating panning
   values, !pan:-1.0/!pan:+1.0); nothing else in the grammar needs a
   signed literal at all. *)
<Value> = TempoMark | Ratio | SignedFloat | SignedInt | StringLit | QualifiedName | StructValue

(* A tempo marking, LilyPond-style: note-value = BPM, e.g. !tempo:4=120
   (quarter note = 120bpm) or !tempo:3/8=120 (dotted quarter = 120bpm --
   a bare Int note-value N means 1/N, an explicit Ratio is taken as-is).
   Only meaningful for the tempo-family keys (tempo/Tempo/T), but parses
   as a generic Value so any assignment could in principle use it. *)
TempoMark = (Int | Ratio) <'='> Int

(* ── Ramp ───────────────────────────────────────────────── *)
(*
   A Ramp is a Value in its own right, but unlike every other Value it
   attaches directly to the assignment name with no colon -- the ramp's
   own leading < or > reads as the separator instead. The curve prefix
   (if any) comes right after the direction, not before it as glued to
   the name -- Name is a greedy letter regex, so a prefix glued onto the
   name (`!vols<...`) is ambiguous about where the name ends; anchored
   after < or >, a letter there can only ever be the curve prefix.
   Four forms:
   - Open-ended, no curve:    !vol<          direction only, no target
   - Open-ended, with curve:  !vol<s         curve set now, target later
   - Timed, no curve:         !vol<16:ff     duration + target
                              !vol<16*4:ff   duration as product
                              !vol<16/1:ff   duration as ratio
                              !pan<4:1.0     float target
                              !tempo>8:60    int target
   - Timed, with curve:       !vol<s:16:ff   curve, duration, target --
                              a ramp point really is this (curve, duration,
                              target) triple; the plain timed form just
                              defaults curve to linear.

   No colon is needed between the curve letter and the duration --
   CurvePrefix is always a single letter and DurationExpr always starts
   with a digit, so the two token classes never collide -- but the timed
   form keeps one for readability, matching a (curve, duration, target)
   triple read left to right. Assignment itself no longer contributes a
   colon for this Value type; the only colon(s) left are internal to Ramp.
*)

Ramp         = RampMark
             | Direction              DurationExpr <':'> Target
             | Direction CurvePrefix <':'> DurationExpr <':'> Target

(* RampMark: direction (+ optional curve), with no value/duration/target
   of its own -- Ramp's own bare, open-ended form, and also (see
   Assignment below) glued directly onto a value in one instruction,
   e.g. !vol:mf< -- sets volume to mf AND marks this point as a ramp-
   start, in one instruction, instead of needing a separate !vol<
   right after. Bare (no '\', unlike Hairpin's own note-suffix '<'/'>')
   since '!' already marks "this is an instruction" -- matches Ramp's
   own existing bare-direction convention (!vol<, !vol<4:160), not
   Hairpin's LilyPond-matching one (see Hairpin/Dynamic below). *)
<RampMark>   = Direction CurvePrefix?

DurationExpr = DurationAtom (<'*'> DurationAtom)*
DurationAtom = Ratio | Int

Target       = DynamicMark | SignedFloat | SignedInt

DynamicMark  = 'pppp' | 'ppp' | 'pp' | 'p' | 'mp' | 'mf'
             | 'ffff' | 'fff' | 'ff' | 'f'

StructValue   = <'('> ws? (ExtendedDataElement (ws ExtendedDataElement)*)? ws? <')'>
QualifiedName = Name (<'.'> Name)*
KeySpec       = #'[A-Ga-g][b#]?(?:\.[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9_]*)*'

<CurvePrefix> = 'l' | 's' | 'i' | 'o'
<Direction>   = '<' | '>'

(* ── Notes ──────────────────────────────────────────────── *)

Note  = Pitch Duration Articulation? NoteSuffix*
      | Pitch           Articulation? NoteSuffix*

(* Split by letter case -- absolute vs. relative resolution
   (leaf-parser/resolve-pitch) already keys off case, and here it's
   load-bearing at the grammar level too: only an absolute (uppercase)
   note may take a bare-digit OctaveAbs (see the comment below), never
   a relative (lowercase) one, where a bare digit must always be a
   Duration -- LilyPond-style relative octave shifts are ' / , ticks
   only, never a digit. This has to be two genuinely different letter
   rules, not one shared PitchLetter both alternatives reference --
   sharing one wouldn't gate anything, since Pitch's own choice of
   OctaveAbs? vs. OctaveTicks? has no way to see which charset a shared
   rule happened to match internally (confirmed by getting this wrong
   the first pass: a single PitchLetter matching either case still let
   OctaveAbs greedily swallow a digit after a lowercase letter, since
   nothing had actually restricted which Pitch alternative applied).
   Nor can a lookbehind on OctaveAbs itself substitute for this --
   instaparse's regex terminals can't see past their own local match at
   all, confirmed directly (a lookbehind on the preceding character
   failed even for the one case it was meant to allow). Splitting the
   letter itself, so each Pitch alternative is only reachable for its
   own case, is the only structural fix. PitchLetterAbs/PitchLetterRel
   tag differently (not both as PitchLetter) -- flat-tree-walker's
   pitch-tuple and the inline :Pitch case check for either tag. *)
Pitch          = PitchLetterAbs Accidental? OctaveAbs?
               | PitchLetterRel Accidental? OctaveTicks?
PitchLetterAbs = #'[A-G]'
PitchLetterRel = #'[a-gp]'

(* Accidental accepts our own symbols (#, b, doubled, n for natural) plus
   EVERY supported \language's own letter-suffix spellings at once --
   LilyPond/Dutch (nederlands, this DSL's default) is/isis (sharp/
   double-sharp), es/eses (flat/double-flat), and the vowel-elided
   s/ses used after a and e (as, ases -- the letter's own trailing vowel
   absorbs the accidental's leading e, e.g. "a"+"es" -> "as", not "aes");
   English's own s/ss/x (sharp/double-sharp -- x is an alternate double-
   sharp spelling) and f/ff (flat/double-flat). This is a genuine
   language UNION at the token-recognition level -- the grammar accepts
   any of these shapes unconditionally, regardless of which language is
   actually active; MEANING is resolved entirely at walk time, by
   common.music-data/accidental-tables keyed off whichever !language:
   is active in the ctx-chain right there (see leaf-parser/
   accidental-semitones and flat-tree-walker/language-for-mode) -- the
   same 'grammar recognizes the shape, walker decides the meaning'
   split :accidentals:implied/explicit already uses, not a parser-level
   language switch (instaparse can't do that mid-file anyway). This is
   exactly why English's own "s" (sharp) and Dutch's own "s" (elided
   flat after a/e) can safely share one token even though they mean
   opposite things: nothing here decides between them, only the walker,
   once it knows which language is actually active. Longer alternatives
   are listed before their own prefixes (isis before is, eses before
   es, ses before s, ss/ff before s/f) since regex alternation commits
   to the first alternative that matches, not the longest -- ss/ff sit
   safely alongside ses (all start with 's'/'f' but diverge at the
   second character, so there's no real prefix conflict between them,
   only within each language's own family). Resolved to a semitone
   offset by leaf-parser/accidental-semitones -- our own internal
   representation stays # / b regardless of which spelling was read. *)
Accidental  = #'isis|eses|ses|ss|ff|is|es|s|f|x|##|bb|[#bn]'

(* An absolute note is always a letter-octave compound -- C4 is octave 4,
   never "C, duration 4" -- so the octave digit is mandatory, but the
   trailing '/' is only there to disambiguate it from an immediately-
   following duration digit (C4/2 = octave 4, duration 2; without the
   slash, C42 would be unparseable as "octave 4, duration 2" -- the
   digits would just run together into Duration's own 1-2-digit
   NumDuration, "octave 4 no duration, duration 42"). Written as
   C4 alone (nothing, or a non-digit, immediately after), the slash
   isn't needed at all and may be omitted. *)
<Octave>    = OctaveAbs | OctaveTicks
OctaveAbs   = #'[1-8](?:/|(?!\d))'
OctaveTicks = #"'+|,+"

(* ── Duration ───────────────────────────────────────────── *)

<Duration>      = DurationSpecial | DurationNum
DurationSpecial = #'\\longa' | #'\\breve'
(* Up to 3 digits, not 2 -- a real, confirmed gap: a 128th note
   (Beethoven's Pathétique, a fast ornamental passage) failed to parse
   at all under the old 2-digit cap, not just round awkwardly; the
   walker's own digit->fraction conversion (flat-tree-walker's
   parse-duration, a plain (/ 1 n)) already handles any N generically,
   so the cap was purely this regex's own, not a computed limit. *)
DurationNum     = #'[1-9][0-9]{0,2}\.*'

(* BareDuration: a duration value with no pitch attached, e.g. /4, /8,
   /16., authored as pure data inside a Data container -- a talea (an
   isorhythmic duration cycle), the durational counterpart of a bare
   Pitch atom (a color/pitch cycle, [c d e f g a b]). Reuses the exact
   same Duration spelling as a Note's own duration digit (DurationNum/
   DurationSpecial) -- no new duration syntax invented, just a leading
   '/' to mark "this atom is a duration alone". The '/' is load-bearing,
   not decorative: a bare digit inside a Data container already means
   Int (DataElement's own Primitive alternative), so an unmarked "4"
   would be ambiguous between Int and Duration -- '/4' can only ever be
   this. *)
BareDuration    = <'/'> Duration

(* ── Articulation ───────────────────────────────────────── *)

Articulation          = <'-'> ArticulationShorthand
                      | <#'\\'> ArticulationName
ArticulationName      = 'staccato' | 'staccatissimo' | 'tenuto' | 'marcato' |
                        'portato'  | 'accent'         | 'espressivo'
ArticulationShorthand = #'[-.>^_!+]'

(* ── Modifiers, Ornaments, Tremolo ──────────────────────── *)

OrnamentName = 'prall'     | 'prallup'      | 'pralldown'   | 'upprall'     |
               'downprall' | 'prallprall'   | 'lineprall'   | 'prallmordent'|
               'mordent'   | 'upmordent'    | 'downmordent' |
               'trill'     | 'turn'         | 'reverseturn' |
               'shortfermata' | 'fermata'   | 'longfermata' | 'verylongfermata'

Ornament = <#'\\'> OrnamentName
Modifier = <#'\\'> Name <':'> ModValue
Tremolo  = <':'> Int

(* A dynamic mark glued directly onto a note/chord, LilyPond-style
   (c4\f, <c e g>4\mf) -- reuses the same DynamicMark rule as Ramp's
   Target, so it's the identical word list a bare !f/!mf BangConst
   accepts. Distinguished from Modifier (which always requires a
   ':' + value) since a bare backslash-name with no colon can only
   ever be this or Ornament/ArticulationName's fixed word lists, none
   of which overlap with DynamicMark's.

   The optional trailing Direction (c4\mf<, no second '\') is the same
   "value + ramp-start in one token" idea as Assignment's own Value
   RampMark? (!vol:mf<) -- chaining a direction glued straight onto the
   mark, not as its own separate Hairpin suffix requiring a second '\'.
   Only the direction, no CurvePrefix -- apply-note-dynamics! (see the
   walker) has never supported a curve here, glued or chained, so this
   doesn't introduce a slot the walker would silently ignore. Bare (no
   '\'), unlike Hairpin's own leading one below, because it's never
   ambiguous with a separate Hairpin suffix: Hairpin always requires its
   own leading '\', so a bare '<'/'>' straight after DynamicMark can only
   ever be this. c4\mf\< (the older two-backslash spelling -- Dynamic
   with no trailing direction, then a separate Hairpin) still parses too,
   unchanged, for exactly that reason -- this isn't a breaking change,
   just a shorter alternative spelling of the same thing. *)
Dynamic  = <#'\\'> DynamicMark Direction?

(* A hairpin start glued onto a note/chord, LilyPond-style (c4\<, c4\>) --
   opens a crescendo/decrescendo from this note onward, same direction-only
   meaning as an open-ended !vol</!vol> Assignment Ramp. Always keeps its
   own leading '\' (unlike Dynamic's trailing Direction above) -- this is
   LilyPond's own spelling for a hairpin (c4\<, c4\>), not something this
   grammar invented, so it stays exactly as LilyPond writes it whether or
   not a Dynamic precedes it. Chainable directly after a Dynamic suffix
   with no separator too (c4\mf\<, the older spelling -- see Dynamic
   above for the newer c4\mf<) since NoteSuffix already repeats freely --
   see apply-note-dynamics! in the walker for how the pair combine either
   way. *)
Hairpin  = <#'\\'> ('<' | '>')

<ModValue>   = Float | Int | StringLit | Name

(* Tremolo is a note/chord suffix: c4:32, <c e g>4:32
   Measured tremolo (\repeat tremolo N seq) is a Command.
   Tie (~) is a NoteSuffix alternative, not a separate trailing Tie? the
   way it used to be -- NoteSuffix* already repeats freely in any order
   (see Hairpin's own comment above), so folding Tie in here lets it
   appear anywhere among a note's suffixes (c4~\prallmordent, same as
   c4\prallmordent~) instead of only ever last. The walker's own tie
   check (flat-tree-walker/has-tie?) already just scans for a :Tie-
   tagged child anywhere in a Note's children, never assumed a fixed
   trailing position, so this needed no walker change at all -- only the
   grammar was actually the constraint. Motivated by a real, confirmed
   case: a note written tie-then-ornament (e4~\prallmordent) failed to
   parse at all under the old Note = ... NoteSuffix* Tie? shape, which
   only accepted ornament-then-tie. *)
<NoteSuffix> = Modifier | Ornament | Dynamic | Hairpin | Tremolo | SlurMark | Tie

(* Slur, LilyPond-style: attaches directly to the note it starts/ends on
   (c4( d4 e4)), not a separate bracketed instruction -- ( ) means ONLY
   this anywhere in the grammar now (Scope, which used to share this
   bracket for \times/\tuplet/\transpose's body and a VarDef's value, is
   gone -- both reuse Sequence's own { } directly, see the top-of-file
   bracket-system comment), except for StructValue's own unrelated '('
   ')' (a parenthesized data list, !key:(...) -- a genuinely different
   construct, still ( )-delimited). No ambiguity with StructValue's own
   parens either: those only ever open at a fresh Value position (always
   after a Sep/ws/a command's own leading tokens), while a SlurMark is
   only reachable glued directly onto a Note/Chord's own trailing
   suffixes, with no separator at all. *)
SlurMark = '(' | ')'

(* ── Tie ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── *)

Tie = '~'

(* ── Chord ───────────────────────────────────────────────── *)

Chord = '<' !'<' ChordPitches '>' Duration Articulation? NoteSuffix*
      | '<' !'<' ChordPitches '>'          Articulation? NoteSuffix*

<ChordPitches> = Pitch ws Pitch (ws Pitch)*

(* ── Rest ────────────────────────────────────────────────── *)

Rest = 'r' Duration
     | 'r'

(* ── MultiRest ───────────────────────────────────────────── *)
(* R, LilyPond's own multi-measure rest -- superset of real LilyPond in
   one specific way (see input.reader.flat-tree-walker/walk-multi-rest):
   with an explicit Duration, this is EXACTLY LilyPond's own R1*4 (the
   composer picks the note-value matching one bar in the current meter,
   same responsibility a bare r's own duration already carries, then
   *n multiplies it) -- real LilyPond doesn't derive this from the
   meter automatically either, despite the name; that's what motivated
   the extension. With NO Duration at all (R, or R*4), this DSL derives
   one bar's length from whatever Meter is actually active at this
   point and multiplies by n -- a real, confirmed transcription bug
   (r1 written meaning "rest one bar" in 3/8 time, where a bare whole-
   note rest is actually ~2.67 bars, not 1) motivated this half; LilyPond
   itself has no equivalent shorthand; it always requires the explicit
   duration. Either way *n defaults to 1 when omitted. *)
MultiRest = 'R' Duration <'*'> Int
          | 'R' Duration
          | 'R' <'*'> Int
          | 'R'

(* ── Drum ────────────────────────────────────────────────── *)

Drum    = 'x' Duration DrumMod?
        | 'x'          DrumMod?
DrumMod = <#'\\'> (Int | Name)

(* ── Primitives ──────────────────────────────────────────── *)

Name        = #'[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9_]*'
Float       = #'[0-9]+\.[0-9]+'
Int         = #'[0-9]+'
Ratio       = #'[0-9]+/[0-9]+'
SignedFloat = #'[+-]?[0-9]+\.[0-9]+'
SignedInt   = #'[+-]?[0-9]+'
StringLit = <'"'> #'[^"]*' <'"'>
Keyword   = <':'> #'[a-zA-Z0-9_][a-zA-Z0-9_\-]*'
Id        = Name <':'>
Reference = <':'> Name

(* ── Whitespace and comments ────────────────────────────── *)
(* LilyPond-style comments only (%.../%{...%}) -- the old ;.../
   (comment ...) forms are gone; nothing in this DSL's own docs or
   examples ever used them, and supporting two unrelated comment
   syntaxes wasn't buying anything.

   Comment is a real, tagged rule (not swallowed silently the way it
   used to be as part of a hidden ws) -- flat-tree-walker discards it,
   same as it already discards bare ws-artifact strings, but because
   nothing is removed from the text before instaparse ever sees it, a
   later parse error's line/column is always a position in what was
   actually written, comments included. ws itself stays hidden and is
   referenced exactly as before everywhere else in this grammar; only
   its own definition changed, so nothing else had to.

   The line-comment alternative excludes a following '{' (negative
   lookahead) so it can never compete with the block-comment
   alternative for the same '%' -- verified directly against instaparse
   that without this, the two are genuinely ambiguous (not just
   "unlikely to be misparsed"): a block comment could otherwise resolve
   to only its first line, silently truncating the rest into further
   bareword/Comment matches instead of being swallowed whole.
   Bar lines '|' are still not part of this -- they're BarLine, matched
   via Sep in Sequence/Parallel (see above), not by ws itself. *)
Comment = #'%\{[\s\S]*?%\}'
        | #'%(?!\{)[^\n]*'
<Blank> = #'\s+'
<ws>    = (Blank | Comment)+